===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The condition or state being [[recrudescent]]; the condition of something (often [[undesirable]]) [[break out|breaking out]] again, or [[reemerge|re-emerging]] after [[temporary]] [[abatement]] or [[suppression]]. {{defdate|from mid 17th c.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|recrudency|q1=archaic|reincrudation|q2=obsolete}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Duke of Argyll [''i.e.'', {{w|George Campbell, 8th Duke of Argyll}}]|chapter=The Age of Covenants|title=Scotland as It Was and as It Is|edition=2nd|location=Edinburgh|publisher=[[w:David Douglas (publisher)|David Douglas]]|year=1887|page=134|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/scotlandasitwasa00argy/page/134/mode/1up|oclc=1061880144|passage=The population of particular countries, or districts of country, may be given up to less improving pursuits than those of agriculture. A '''recrudescence''' of barbarism may condemn it [''i.e.'', land] to chronic poverty and waste.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=James U. Van Dyke|chapter=Cues for Reproduction in Squamate Reptiles|editors=Justin L. Rheubert, Dustin S. Siegel, and {{w|Stanley E. Trauth}}|title=Reproductive Biology and Phylogeny of Lizards and Tuatara|series=Reproductive Biology and Phylogeny|seriesvolume=10|location=Boca Raton, Fla.|publisher={{w|CRC Press}}|year=2015|page=119|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=v4zNBQAAQBAJ&pg=PA119|isbn=978-1-4665-7986-6|passage=High serum melatonin concentrations generally suppress reproduction in male sqamates. Pinealectomy, which eliminates most melatonin secretion, induces testicular '''recrudescence''' in male green anoles, ''Anolis carolinensis'', in autumn, but not in summer{{nb...}}. Injected melatonin does not inhibit testicular '''recrudescence''' in pinealectomized males, but does so in intact males{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine|by extension}} The [[acute#Adjective|acute]] [[recurrence]] of a [[disease]], or its [[symptom]]s, after a period of [[improvement]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=J. L. Bardsley|entry=HYDROPHOBIA|editors=[[w:John Forbes (physician)|John Forbes]], {{w|Alexander Tweedie}}, and {{w|John Conolly}}|title=The Cyclopædia of Practical Medicine;{{nb...|Comprising Treatises on the Nature and Treatment of Diseases, Materia Medica and Therapeutics, Medical Jurisprudence, etc. etc.}}|location=London|publisher=Sherwood, Gilbert, and Piper; Baldwin and Cradock,{{nb...|Paternoster-Row}}; Whittaker, Treacher, and Co.,{{nb...|Ave-Maria-Lane.}}|year=1833|volume=II (EME–ISC)|page=484|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=umsSAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA484|column=1|oclc=1012799552|passage=In other cases the wound opens of itself, and discharges a peculiar matter. Something similar is known to take place in traumatic tetanus; and although in this affection, as well as in hydrophobia, we cannot explain why the phenomenon of '''recrudescence''' does not occur in many fatal examples, yet we ought not therefore to deny that in those cases in which it does appear, the connexion between the '''recrudescence''' and the disease is most remarkable.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Charles Alexander Gordon|chapter=Recurring Epidemics; Recrudescence|title=Notes on the Hygiene of Cholera for Ready Reference|location=London|publisher=Bailliere, Tindall, & Cox; Madras: Gantz Brothers,{{nb...|7 and 8, Mount Road}}; Bombay; Calcutta: Thacker & Co.|year=1877|section=paragraph 5|page=10|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=PBgDAAAAQAAJ&pg=RA1-PA10|oclc=14858340|passage=It becomes very difficult under many circumstances to distinguish between an epidemic solely due to '''''recrudescence''''' of the cholera principle retained from previous outbreak, and an epidemic the result of fresh introduction.}}
# {{lb|en|botany}} The [[production]] of a [[fresh]] [[shoot#Noun|shoot]] from a [[ripened#Adjective|ripened]] [[spike#Noun|spike]].

